INVALID, stopped at 39 of 60 pairs. No score is reported from it.

Ten of its 78 completed games, 12.8%, ended in an agent missing the platform's 90 second
import budget: six `both_failed` and, worse, four `init`. A `both_failed` is symmetric and
merely dilutes. An `init` is one-sided, and hands the opponent a win by default, so this run
was biased and not just noisy. Pair 29 scored 2.0/2 with one of its games decided that way.

Cause, identified while it ran rather than guessed at afterwards: Steam's `fossilize_replay`
shader pre-caching had three processes at about 93% of a core each, with Spotify and a zstd
decompression alongside, taking the machine to a load average of 13 on six physical cores.
An agent import that takes 57 seconds alone does not fit in 90 seconds against that. Nothing
about either engine is implicated; the same three-worker setting played 240 games with zero
failures the previous evening on a quieter machine.

It was stopped rather than finished because it could not produce a usable number and the
cores were better left free. It is not re-run: the deadline is too close for another
two-hour match, and the recommendation does not depend on it. The independent move-quality
measurement in `compare-nets-run2.json`, which does not need a quiet machine, had already
replicated the previous run's finding that this network is slightly worse in search.

The general lesson, which is the same one the previous handoff recorded and this run has now
paid for twice: this machine is not dedicated, and a wall-time match must be run when it is
quiet or not at all.
